!PCSX - PCSX ReARMed for RISC OS                    @VERSION@
================================

PCSX ReARMed is a Sony PlayStation emulator, written for ARM machines.
This is its libretro core with a native RISC OS desktop frontend: a
window you can drag disc images onto, full screen, sound through
SharedSound, the keyboard and USB game pads.

If something goes wrong, PCSX can write a log to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.pcsxlog:
take the | off the "Set PCSX$LogOn 1" line in !PCSX.!Run, run PCSX
again, and the icon bar menu's "Log..." opens it. Please include it when
reporting a problem.


Requirements
------------
- RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine with NEON (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4, 400,
  500; most Cortex-A8/A9 boards). Written for and best on a Pi 4.
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16+ and ARMEABISupport (PackMan installs both).
- For sound: SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager (ssb.zip from
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html). Without them,
  games run silently.
- For game pads: !USBJoystick (USBJoystick 0.23 or later), run before
  PCSX or from your boot sequence.


Games library
-------------
Clicking !PCSX's icon bar icon opens the games library (Adjust opens the
game's window). Drag folders of disc images into it: PCSX looks through
them, and the folders inside them, for games, and lists each one once (a
.cue and its .bin are one game, so are the discs in an .m3u; a zip or 7z
with a game in it is one too, and its serial is read while the computer
is otherwise idle). Every game
you play is added too, and the five you played last are at the top.

Each game shows its title (the file's name), its region and serial (read
from the disc: SLES/SCES = PAL, SLUS/SCUS = NTSC-U, SLPS/SCPS = NTSC-J),
when you last played it, and how many save states it has.

Double-click a game, or select it and press Return, to play it. The
library's menu has:
  Play
  Game settings   High resolution, Dynamic recompiler, Built-in BIOS
                  only, Full screen, Pad 1 type: kept for that game only
                  (in <Choices$Write>.PCSX.games) and used whenever it
                  starts; "Use the usual settings" forgets them
  Remove from library
  Sort            by name or last played first
  Rescan folders  after adding or removing games
  Folders         remove a folder (its games go, unless played)
  Show covers

Covers are JPEG pictures, drawn by RISC OS (SpriteExtend). PCSX uses, in
this order: a JPEG with the disc's name next to the disc (Game/jpg beside
Game/cue), one in a "covers" folder beside it, one in
<Choices$Write>.PCSX.covers named after the serial (SLES02574/jpg), or
after the disc's name. Square-ish pictures of about 200 pixels look best.


Playing
-------
Drag a disc image onto the icon bar icon, the library or the game window,
or double-click it in the library. PCSX reads:
  .cue with .bin    (the usual rip of a CD; keep them together)
  .chd              (compressed, from MAME's chdman)
  .pbp              (PSP "eboot" images)
  .iso, .img
  .m3u              (a list of discs for multi-disc games)
  .exe              (PlayStation programs, e.g. homebrew)
  .zip, .7z         any of these, packed (see below)

Zip and 7z files: PCSX unpacks the game into <Wimp$ScrapDir>.PCSXGame
(the window's title shows how far it has got; close the window to stop),
plays it from there, and deletes it when you close the game, play
another, or quit. A CD's worth takes a little while to unpack, and needs
that much free disc space; playing the same game again straight away
doesn't unpack it again. The desktop keeps running while it unpacks.
Save states, memory cards and per-game settings go by the archive's name.
Zip: stored, deflated or LZMA (zip64 too). 7z: LZMA or LZMA2 (7-Zip's
usual settings), solid or not; others only in small blocks. Encrypted
archives aren't supported. PCSX opens zips itself, so it doesn't matter
whether SparkFS is loaded.

A file with a space in its name is shown by RISC OS with a hard space;
.cue files that name such files are understood.

Click in the window so it has the input focus (the keyboard is only read
then). Menu over the window: Pause, Reset, Full screen, Scale, save
states and slots, Close game, Quit.

Keys (change them in Choices):
  Cursor keys     D-pad
  Z  X  A  S      Cross  Circle  Square  Triangle
  Q  W  1  2      L1  R1  L2  R2      3  4   L3  R3
  Return          Start              Right Shift   Select

  F2 / F4         save / load the state in the current slot
  F6 / F7         previous / next slot (0-9)
  F8              pause
  F11, Alt-Return full screen on and off; Escape leaves full screen
  F12             (as always: the command line)

Full screen is a window covering the screen, so the desktop keeps
running underneath; the menu still works there.


BIOS
----
PCSX has a built-in replacement for the PlayStation BIOS (HLE), which
runs most games. A real BIOS image works better with some games and
shows the boot screen; if you have one from your own console, put it in
<Choices$Write>.PCSX.bios (e.g. scph1001/bin or scph5501/bin). It is
used automatically.


Memory cards and save states
----------------------------
Memory card 1 is <Choices$Write>.PCSX.saves.pcsx-card1/mcd, shared by
all games, as on a real console; card 2 is pcsx-card2/mcd (other choices
in Choices). Save states go in <Choices$Write>.PCSX.states.<game>.


Game pads
---------
With !USBJoystick running, the first two pads are players 1 and 2. A hat
switch or D-pad is the PlayStation D-pad. Analogue sticks reach the game
if you set pad1.type to analog or dualshock in Choices. Pads differ in
how they number their buttons: set joy.log to 1, press each button, and
read the numbers in the log; then set joy.buttons to match.

Pads are read with Joystick_Read, the joystick API in the PRM, which
works with any Joystick module. USBJoystick's newer calls, which give
the hat, the right stick and the pad's name, are used as well when
they're there. They're still a draft, so if they ever don't answer as
expected PCSX falls back to Joystick_Read (and says so in the log).
"joy.api: stable" in Choices uses Joystick_Read alone.


Choices
-------
The icon bar menu's "Choices..." opens <Choices$Write>.PCSX.Choices.
!PCSX.Resources.Choices lists every setting and what it does; copy the
lines you want to change into your Choices and choose "Reload choices".
Window scale, sound latency, keys, pad types, joystick buttons, and the
emulator's own options (dynamic recompiler on or off, BIOS, region,
frame skipping, high resolution rendering, ...) are all there.


Speed
-----
The dynamic recompiler and the NEON renderer are what make it fast. With
the log on, the title bar shows the emulated frame rate: 60, or 50 for a
PAL game, means full speed. If a game is too slow, try
pcsx_rearmed_frameskip_type: auto. If a game misbehaves, try
pcsx_rearmed_drc: disabled (much slower) to see if the recompiler is to
blame.


